WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.119 --> 00:00:03.500
Kubernetes has never once
started a container.

2
00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:04.239
Not

3
00:00:04.739 --> 00:00:05.099
one.

4
00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.380
Yet everyone credits it for the
whole show.

5
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.619
Docker takes your code, stuffs
it into a tidy little box called

6
00:00:12.659 --> 00:00:13.500
a container,

7
00:00:13.859 --> 00:00:15.379
and can run that box

8
00:00:15.819 --> 00:00:18.979
right there on one single
machine.

9
00:00:19.459 --> 00:00:21.819
That part everyone gets right.

10
00:00:22.139 --> 00:00:25.739
But what happens when you have
four hundred of these boxes

11
00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:28.139
scattered across sixty machines?

12
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.099
Who decides which box goes
where?

13
00:00:31.479 --> 00:00:35.799
Who notices when one dies in the
night and quietly wheels in a

14
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:36.899
replacement?

15
00:00:37.319 --> 00:00:38.819
Docker has no idea.

16
00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:42.219
Docker only knows the one box in
front of it.

17
00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:44.180
That's Kubernetes,

18
00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:48.719
a great glowing brain in the
sky, deciding placement,

19
00:00:48.819 --> 00:00:49.919
counting replicas,

20
00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:53.479
rerouting traffic, resurrecting
the dead.

21
00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:55.239
Magnificent.

22
00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:56.379
Except,

23
00:00:56.959 --> 00:00:59.439
and here's the part that keeps
me up at night,

24
00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.439
Kubernetes has no hands.

25
00:01:02.859 --> 00:01:05.500
It cannot start a container
itself.

26
00:01:05.779 --> 00:01:10.139
It simply barks an order at a
small assistant living on each

27
00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:14.619
machine, and that assistant,
using the same container engine

28
00:01:14.699 --> 00:01:15.559
Docker uses,

29
00:01:15.939 --> 00:01:18.000
does the actual starting.

30
00:01:18.279 --> 00:01:21.059
So Kubernetes doesn't replace
Docker.

31
00:01:21.339 --> 00:01:22.699
It never did.

32
00:01:22.959 --> 00:01:27.139
It just stands on a balcony
pointing while somebody else's

33
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.159
hands do the work and somehow
gets all of the credit.

34
00:01:31.599 --> 00:01:33.319
Typical management.

35
00:01:33.659 --> 00:01:34.659
Good night, everyone.

36
00:01:35.623 --> 00:01:38.563
Comment Kubernetes and I'll send
you the diagram.

37
00:01:38.903 --> 00:01:40.743
Don't make me repeat myself
